Looking at this task, I need to implement a RIGHT JOIN between Department and Project tables. However, SQLite doesn't natively support RIGHT JOIN, so I'll need to implement it as a LEFT JOIN with the tables reversed.

Based on the code fragments showing the sqlgen API patterns, here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#pragma once

#include <sqlgen.hpp>
#include <sqlgen/sqlite.hpp>
#include <vector>
#include <optional>

namespace right_join_departments_projects {

struct Department {
  sqlgen::PrimaryKey<int> department_id;
  std::string name;
};

struct Project {
  sqlgen::PrimaryKey<int> project_id;
  int department_id;
  std::string name;
};

struct DepProj {
  std::string department_name;
  std::optional<std::string> project_name;
};

/**
 * This function demonstrates how to perform a right join between departments and projects using sqlgen read API.
 * Returns: std::vector<DepProj>, where each struct contains the department name (if available) and the project name.
 */
std::vector<DepProj> right_join_departments_projects() {
  using namespace sqlgen;
  using namespace sqlgen::literals;

  // SQLite doesn't support RIGHT JOIN natively, so we use LEFT JOIN with reversed tables
  // RIGHT JOIN Project with Department = LEFT JOIN Department with Project
  // This ensures all departments are included, even if they have no projects

  auto conn = sqlgen::sqlite::connect("test_db.sqlite");

  if (!conn) {
    return {};
  }

  // Custom query to perform LEFT JOIN from Department to Project
  // This gives us all departments with their associated projects (or NULL if no project)
  const std::string query = R"(
    SELECT d.name AS department_name, p.name AS project_name
    FROM Department d
    LEFT JOIN Project p ON d.department_id = p.department_id
  )";

  auto result = conn.and_then([&query](auto& db) {
    return db.template execute<std::vector<DepProj>>(query);
  });

  if (result) {
    return result.value();
  }

  return {};
}

}  // namespace right_join_departments_projects
```

This implementation:

1. **Connects to the SQLite database** "test_db.sqlite"
2. **Performs a LEFT JOIN** from Department to Project (which simulates a RIGHT JOIN from Project to Department, since SQLite doesn't support RIGHT JOIN)
3. **Ensures all departments are included** in the result, even if they have no associated projects (project_name will be `std::nullopt` in those cases)
4. **Returns the results** as a `std::vector<DepProj>` containing department names and optional project names

The key insight is that `RIGHT JOIN Project WITH Department` is equivalent to `LEFT JOIN Department WITH Project`, which ensures every department appears in the results.

